.TH SM 5
.SH NAME
sm \- sheet metal part source
.SH DESCRIPTION
A part source for
.IR smfold (1)
is written in the language of
.IR plicad (7),
which gives its comments, definitions, includes and numbers.
Flat coordinates are x and y; the sheet occupies z in [\-t, 0], and its
near face, z = 0, is the reference face.
Statements are applied in order.
Each statement that makes a feature gives it a NAME, new in the part:
the plate, a flange, a cut, a hole, an outline or a trim.
Each edge and hole of the blank carries the name of the feature that
made it, as the drawings select by it
.RI ( draw (5)).
.TP
.BI sheet " t r k"
Thickness, inside bend radius, k-factor.
.TP
.BI plate " name x0 y0 x1 y1 " at " ox oy oz " axes " u v"
The root slab.
Flat x and y run along the 3D axes
.I u
and
.I v
(+x, \-z, ...); the near face faces
.IR u " \(mu " v ,
and flat (0, 0) is at
.RI ( ox ,
.IR oy ,
.IR oz ).
.TP
.BI flange " name parent dir angle leg"
.RB [ at
.IR c ]
.RB [ span
.IR "a b" ]
.RB [ zone
.IR "a b" ]
.br
A bend zone, then a straight leg, on the edge of
.I parent
in flat direction
.I dir
(+x \-x +y \-y), or on the line where that coordinate is
.IR c .
A positive angle, in degrees, folds toward the near face.
.I Span
is the extent along the edge (default that of the parent);
.I zone
that of the bend (default the overlap of span and parent).
.TP
.BI cut " name u0 v0 u1 v1 " along " a"
.RB [ from
.I w0
.B to
.IR w1 ]
.br
A rectangle cut through every slab so far that lies across the 3D axis
.I a
.RI ( u
and
.I v
are the other two axes, in alphabetical order),
or only through those between
.I w0
and
.I w1
along
.IR a .
.TP
.BI hole " name d u v " along " a"
.RB [ from
.I w0
.B to
.IR w1 ]
.RB [ csink
.I dc angle
.BR near | far ]
.RB [ pattern
.I ax n p
.RI [ "ax n p" ]
|
.B pattern hex
.I p
.B within
.IR r ]
.br
A hole of diameter
.IR d ,
as for a cut.
A pattern repeats it
.I n
times,
.I p
apart along the signed axis
.IR ax ;
a hex pattern fills the circle of radius
.I r
with a hexagonal grid.
A countersink of diameter
.I dc
and included angle
.I angle
opens the hole at the near face or the far one.
.TP
.BI insert " name type " near | far
.RB [ length
.IR l ]
.RB [ as
.IR body ]
.I u v
.B along
.I a
.RB [ from
.I w0
.B to
.IR w1 ]
.RB [ pattern
.I ax n p
.RI [ "ax n p" ]]
.br
Self-clinching hardware of the type, a row of the table inserts next
to the source or else of the library
.RI ( plicad-lib (7)),
in its mounting hole, which the statement cuts as a hole statement
cuts one, with the pattern as for a hole, and names
.IR name .
The hole goes through the sheet, or for a blind type into it from the
face the insert stands on, as deep as the type says.
Its body, a cylinder of the size of the type, or with
.B length
of that height, stands on the near face or the far one over the hole;
the body of a flush or a blind type fills the hole too.
A sheet thinner than the type allows is refused, and so is a hole whose
center lies nearer an edge or a bend line of the blank than the type
allows.
The inserts are named
.IR body ,
or else
.IR name ,
and those of a pattern NAME, NAME-2 and on; their bodies are the parts
PART/NAME of the stream, while the drawings name the hole
.IR name .
.TP
.BI outline " name " along " a"
.RB [ from
.I w0
.B to
.IR w1 ]
.br
.BI point " u v"
.RB [ arc
.I cu cv
.BR ccw | cw ]
.br
A hole of any outline, as for a cut, given by the points that follow,
each joined to the next by a line or an arc about
.RI ( cu ,
.IR cv ).
.TP
.BI trim " name " along " a"
.RB [ from
.I w0
.B to
.IR w1 ]
.br
An open curve of points from the outline of a slab face to another
point of it; the smaller piece is cut off.
.TP
.BI round " r name"
.RB [ at
.IR "x y z" ]
.br
Round the two free corners of flange
.IR name ,
or the corner of slab
.I name
at the point (a round if convex, a fillet if concave).
The arcs carry the name of the slab.
.TP
.BI coat " material"
.BR near | far
.RB [ edges ]
.I region
.br
A material on one side of the sheet, put on the flat blank before it
is formed: the near face or the far one, and with
.B edges
also the cut edges and the holes of the blank in the region.
.I Material
names a material of the assembly
.RI ( assembly (5));
where no coat lies the part keeps the material of its assembly line.
The region is one of
.RS
.TP
.B all
the whole blank;
.TP
.BI flat " x0 y0 x1 y1"
.RI [ "x2 y2" ] ...
.br
a rectangle of two corners, or a polygon of three or more, in the flat
coordinates of the blank, as the plate takes them; such a region may
reach across bends;
.TP
.IB "u0 v0 u1 v1 " along " a"
.RB [ from
.I w0
.B to
.IR w1 ]
.br
a rectangle across the 3D axis
.IR a ,
as for a cut, on every slab square to
.I a
that it meets, each up to the edges of that slab: so a band on a wall
also marks a flange beside the wall that the rectangle reaches, unless
.B from
and
.B to
hold it to the wall alone.
.RE
.IP
Coats come in order, a later one over an earlier one where they
overlap.
A coat has no thickness: it changes neither the solid nor its mass.
A coat of a finish with a texture
.RI ( plicad (7))
shows on a drawing as a region with its border and its mark, and its
callout goes into the notes
.RI ( draw (5)).
.TP
.BI material " ..."
.br
.BI materials " file"
Materials for the coats, as
.IR plicad (7)
says, which go into the stream of the part, for its drawing.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
sheet 2 2 0.44
plate base 0 0 100 50 at 0 0 0 axes +x +y
flange wall base +y 90 30
round 5 wall
hole center 6 50 25 along z
hole pins 4 20 20 along y pattern x 3 30
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
plicad(7), smfold(1), assembly(5), stream(5)
